    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now honors an optional `Idempotency-Key` header: a repeat create with an already-seen key returns the originally created owner with **200**, instead of building a duplicate (which the existing identity check would reject with 409).\n\nI kept each piece of logic in its own small unit and reused the codebase's existing mechanisms (escalation-based short-circuit, `Out`/`@Val` state, Spring-bean injection):\n\n**New pieces**\n- `IdempotencyStore` (`@Component`) \u2014 the single home for idempotency knowledge: the header name, extracting the key from the request (`ServerHttpConnection`), and an in-memory `key \u2192 ownerId` map. It's intentionally non-transactional so a replay is recognized across the request (and transaction) that first stored it.\n- `CheckIdempotency` \u2014 new **first** step. If the key was seen before, it throws `IdempotentReplayException(ownerId)`, short-circuiting *before* any validation/build/persist. New or absent key \u2192 pipeline proceeds unchanged.\n- `RecordIdempotencyKey` \u2014 runs after `save` (id assigned), recording `key \u2192 owner.getId()`; a no-op when no key was sent.\n- `IdempotentReplayException` + its handler \u2014 the handler loads the original owner and responds 200 with the owner DTO, using the escalation mechanism already used throughout this codebase (`NotFound`, `DuplicateIdentity`, etc.).\n\n**Wiring**\n- `owners.POST.yml`: added `checkIdempotency` as the entry step (`next: validate`) and `recordIdempotency` between `resolveBulkWarning` and `respond`.\n- New escalation YAML mapping `IdempotentReplayException` to its handler (most-specific match, so it takes precedence over the generic `java.lang.Exception` handler).\n\n**Why escalation rather than a `@Flow` branch:** it's the pattern this codebase actually uses for \"respond and stop the pipeline,\" so the change stays consistent with the surrounding code. Owner\u2192pets is `FetchType.EAGER`, so the handler safely maps the loaded owner even though it runs outside the write-transaction governance.\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` passes (same id returned, second call is 200), and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
